<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Epirus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Epirus is a mountainous region of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindus">Pindus</a> range and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Sea</a>, divided today between north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and southern Albania. In antiquity it was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a> and other Greek-speaking tribes, whose kingdom reached its height under Pyrrhus. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it formed the core of the Despotate of Epirus, and later of Ali Pasha's domain centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epirus.jpg" alt="Epirus" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Kiepert · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epirus_antiquus_tabula.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…its Balkan neighbours, shaped the wars that ended Ottoman rule in Europe, and its territory grew from the narrow state of 1832 to include the Ionian Islands (1864), Thessaly and Arta (1881), Macedonia, <b>Epirus</b>, Crete and the eastern Aegean islands (1912–13), Western Thrace (1920) and the Dodecanese (1947).</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom first lay along the Haliacmon and Loudias rivers north of Mount Olympus and along the coast of the Thermaic Gulf, bordered by <b>Epirus</b>, Illyria, Paeonia, Thrace and Thessaly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Squeezed between the Despotate of the Morea, Venice, the Tocco rulers of <b>Epirus</b> and Ottoman raiders, he lost ground steadily.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Asen II (r. 1218–1241) won the Battle of Klokotnitsa on 9 March 1230, capturing Theodore Komnenos Doukas, ruler of <b>Epirus</b> and Thessalonica, and bringing much of Thrace, Macedonia and Albania under Bulgarian rule, as he proclaimed in an inscription in the Church of the Forty Martyrs in Tarnovo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman tradition held that the kings of early Rome were chosen by the people’s assemblies after an interregnum, and in Macedon and <b>Epirus</b>, according to ancient writers, the assembled army acclaimed new kings.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>King Manfred of Sicily married Helena Angelina Doukaina, daughter of Michael II Komnenos Doukas, Despot of <b>Epirus</b>, as an expression of their alliance against the power of the Empire of Nicaea.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-2">2 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
